## Claim
Text: "Republican Matt Gaetz was part of a company that had to pay 75 million in hospice fraud. They stole from dying people."
Claim date: October 31, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What is the name of the company with which Matt Gaetz was allegedly associated in the hospice fraud case?
Answer: The company is Vitas Healthcare Corporation, formerly owned by Matt Gaetz's father, Don Gaetz.

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/nov/01/facebook-posts/post-says-rep-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-wrong/

### What specific role or relationship did Matt Gaetz have with that company (e.g., owner, investor, board member, or employee)?
Answer: Based on the search result, Matt Gaetz had no specific role or relationship with the hospice company involved in the fraud case. The company, Vitas Healthcare Corporation, was previously owned by his father, Don Gaetz, and Matt Gaetz was not named as a defendant, owner, investor, board member, or employee in connection with it. The source explicitly states, "It didn’t involve Matt Gaetz."

Source URL: https://www.tampabay.com/florida-politics/buzz/2019/11/04/politifact-facebook-post-says-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-its-wrong/

### Did the company pay a $75 million penalty, settlement, or judgment specifically for hospice fraud?
Answer: Yes, the company (Vitas) paid a settlement of more than $75 million in 2017 to resolve allegations of defrauding Medicare through false claims for hospice services, which constitutes hospice fraud. The source states that the Justice Department sued Vitas for allegedly filing "false claims for services never provided or for patients who weren’t terminally ill," and the settlement was "the largest settlement ever recovered from a hospice company."

Source URL: https://www.pnj.com/story/opinion/2019/07/20/marlette-mother-jones-kicks-open-gaetz/1761716001/

### What legal authority (e.g., Department of Justice, state court, or federal agency) imposed the $75 million payment on the company?
Answer: The $75 million payment was imposed by the U.S. Justice Department, which filed the lawsuit and later settled the case. The source states: "In 2013, the U.S. Justice Department filed a lawsuit against Chemed Corporation and hospice subsidiaries... In 2017, the government settled the case for $75 million."

Source URL: https://www.tampabay.com/florida-politics/buzz/2019/11/04/politifact-facebook-post-says-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-its-wrong/

### Did the legal case or settlement involve allegations that the company defrauded hospice patients or their families?
Answer: The search result describes a lawsuit alleging that Vitas Hospice Services defrauded the Medicare program by billing for ineligible patients and unnecessary services, but it does not indicate that the company directly defrauded hospice patients or their families. The allegations focus on overcharging the government, not stealing from dying individuals or their relatives.

Source URL: https://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/legislature/senate-president-don-gaetzs-former-company-sued-for-medicaid-fraud/2120026/

### Was Matt Gaetz ever named as a defendant, respondent, or party in any lawsuit or investigation related to that hospice fraud?
Answer: The search result does not mention Matt Gaetz as a defendant, respondent, or party in any lawsuit or investigation related to the hospice fraud; it only discusses his father, Don Gaetz, as a founder and former vice chairman of Vitas Hospice.

Source URL: https://www.deseret.com/2013/5/9/20451919/largest-us-hospice-company-sued-for-medicare-fraud

### Is there any documented evidence (e.g., court records, news articles, or government reports) linking Matt Gaetz directly to the fraudulent conduct?
Answer: No, the search result does not provide any documented evidence linking Matt Gaetz directly to the fraudulent conduct. The article discusses a lawsuit against Vitas Hospice, mentioning that the company was founded by Don Gaetz (Matt Gaetz’s father) and that Don Gaetz served as vice chairman of the board during the alleged fraud period, with no reference to Matt Gaetz’s involvement.

Source URL: https://www.deseret.com/2013/5/9/20451919/largest-us-hospice-company-sued-for-medicare-fraud

### When did the alleged hospice fraud occur, and when was the $75 million payment made?
Answer: The alleged hospice fraud occurred between 2002 and 2013, as the government accused the company of engaging in Medicare fraud during that period. The $75 million payment was made in 2017 when the government settled the case.

Source URL: https://www.tampabay.com/florida-politics/buzz/2019/11/04/politifact-facebook-post-says-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-its-wrong/

### Has any reputable fact-checking organization (e.g., PolitiFact, FactCheck.org, or Snopes) investigated this claim and, if so, what were their conclusions?
Answer: Yes, PolitiFact investigated this claim and concluded it is false. Their fact-check, published by the Tampa Bay Times, found that Matt Gaetz had only one DUI arrest (not "numerous"), and the case was dropped; the hospice fraud involved a company previously owned by his father, Don Gaetz, and neither Matt nor Don Gaetz was named as a defendant in the $75 million settlement. The post was rated as wrong.

Source URL: https://www.tampabay.com/florida-politics/buzz/2019/11/04/politifact-facebook-post-says-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-its-wrong/

## Final Judgement
The fact-check evidence clearly shows that the company involved in the $75 million hospice fraud settlement is Vitas Healthcare, which was owned by Matt Gaetz's father, Don Gaetz. Matt Gaetz had no role as an owner, investor, board member, or employee of that company, and he was never named as a defendant or party in the lawsuit. The settlement was for defrauding Medicare, not directly stealing from patients. PolitiFact has rated a similar claim as false. Therefore, the claim that Matt Gaetz was "part of" the company that committed hospice fraud is explicitly contradicted by the evidence.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is false: the $75 million hospice fraud settlement involved Vitas Healthcare, a company previously owned by Matt Gaetz's father, Don Gaetz, but Matt Gaetz had no role as an owner, investor, board member, or employee, and was never named as a defendant in the lawsuit ([PolitiFact](https://www.tampabay.com/florida-politics/buzz/2019/11/04/politifact-facebook-post-says-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-its-wrong/)). The settlement resolved allegations of defrauding Medicare, not directly stealing from patients ([PNJ](https://www.pnj.com/story/opinion/2019/07/20/marlette-mother-jones-kicks-open-gaetz/1761716001/)). PolitiFact rated a similar claim as false.